WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Atomics and mutexes solve different problems.

2
00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:09.596
Atomics are useful for simple independent state, such as a stop flag, readiness flag, metrics counter, or tightly designed state transition.

3
00:00:09.596 --> 00:00:12.440
Mutexes are often clearer when protecting compound invariants.

4
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:21.681
If several fields must be read or updated together, a container and its metadata must stay consistent, or ownership changes need to be observed as a

5
00:00:21.681 --> 00:00:26.656
transaction, a small critical section may be easier to reason about than several atomics.

6
00:00:26.656 --> 00:00:29.855
Using atomics everywhere can make code harder to review.

7
00:00:29.855 --> 00:00:36.608
A class with many atomic fields may look synchronized, but the relationships among those fields may still be unclear.

8
00:00:36.608 --> 00:00:41.229
The design can become a set of independent updates with no obvious invariant.

9
00:00:41.229 --> 00:00:43.006
Performance assumptions should be challenged.

10
00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:46.915
Lock-free does not automatically mean faster, safer, or more maintainable.

11
00:00:46.915 --> 00:00:50.470
Contention, retry loops, cache effects, and team expertise all matter.

12
00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:51.891
Mutexes also communicate intent.

13
00:00:51.891 --> 00:00:56.867
A lock around a protected block says that the block preserves a shared invariant.

14
00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:02.554
That can be more readable than a custom atomic protocol when the invariant spans multiple values.

15
00:01:02.554 --> 00:01:05.753
The secure engineering choice is the clearest correct synchronization.

16
00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:10.373
If an atomic makes the state transition simple and testable, use it deliberately.

17
00:01:10.373 --> 00:01:14.283
If a mutex makes the invariant obvious, prefer the boring design.

18
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:17.837
During review, ask whether the atomic value is truly independent.

19
00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:25.301
If the answer depends on another object, pointer, buffer, or lifecycle rule, consider whether the code needs a broader synchronization boundary.

20
00:01:25.301 --> 00:01:31.343
This keeps the synchronization choice aligned with the invariant instead of with a preference for one primitive.

21
00:01:31.343 --> 00:01:33.120
Correctness should lead the choice.
